# {{ name }} (TWA)

Generated by [WebView](https://github.com/rupok/webview). This wraps
**{{ url }}** as a [Trusted Web Activity](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/android/trusted-web-activity)
— Google's blessed path for shipping a PWA on the Play Store.

## Build

You need [Bubblewrap](https://github.com/GoogleChromeLabs/bubblewrap) (Node):

```bash
npm install -g @bubblewrap/cli
bubblewrap init --manifest=./twa-manifest.json
bubblewrap build
```

Output: `app-release-bundle.aab` (Play Store) and `app-release-signed.apk`
(sideload).

## Digital Asset Links

For the TWA to launch full-screen (instead of falling back to a Chrome
custom tab) you must publish a Digital Asset Links file at
`https://{{ host }}/.well-known/assetlinks.json` that pairs your site
with your APK signing key.

After your first successful `bubblewrap build`, run:

```bash
bubblewrap fingerprint
```

…and add the resulting fingerprint to the assetlinks file shown there.
Without this step, the URL bar will be visible at the top of the app.

## Updating

Re-run `webview build --twa --output .` whenever `webview.json` changes.

## Why TWA over a plain WebView?

- Renders with the user's actual Chrome (full Chrome features, not a
  stripped-down WebView).
- Shares cookies, autofill, password manager with Chrome.
- Auto-updates Chrome means the rendering engine is always current.
- Play Store accepts it without complaint about WebView wrappers.

If your site is **not** a PWA, use `webview build --kotlin` instead.
